# Application Protocols and Network Security: How the Web Talks and Stays Safe

> Above the network plumbing sit the protocols people actually meet —
> HTTP, DNS, email, DHCP — and the threats that target them. This
> lesson covers how they work and how to defend them.

## 1. HTTP: The Language of the Web

**HTTP** is a stateless request-response protocol: the client asks,
the server answers, the server forgets.

| Method | Meaning | Body |
| --- | --- | --- |
| GET | fetch a resource; must not change anything | no |
| POST | submit data, create something | yes |
| PUT | replace a resource | yes |
| DELETE | remove a resource | no |
| HEAD | headers only, no body | no |

| Status family | Meaning | Famous members |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1xx | informational | 101 Switching Protocols |
| 2xx | success | 200 OK, 204 No Content |
| 3xx | redirect | 301 Moved Permanently |
| 4xx | client error | 404 Not Found, 403 Forbidden |
| 5xx | server error | 500 Internal Server Error |

[info title="Why stateless scales"]
Because no server remembers any request, any of thousands of
interchangeable machines can answer any request. State lives in
cookies and tokens — small strings the browser echoes back on every
visit after the server sets them via Set-Cookie.
[/info]

## 2. HTTPS and TLS

**HTTPS** is HTTP inside a **TLS** tunnel. During the handshake the
parties agree on encryption, and the server presents a **certificate**
— a signed statement binding its domain to its public key. The browser
verifies the chain of trust:

@diagram tree id="certificate-chain" layout="tree" aria="Certificate chain from a trusted root CA through an intermediate to the site certificate"
  "Root CA (preinstalled in your OS)" -> "Intermediate CA"
  "Intermediate CA" -> "Site certificate for example.com"
@/diagram

The padlock icon means: encrypted in transit, and the certificate was
verified against a **Certificate Authority (CA)** your system already
trusts. **HTTP/2** multiplexes many requests over one connection;
**HTTP/3** runs on QUIC, built over UDP, for faster handshakes.

## 3. DNS: Names to Numbers

| Record | Holds |
| --- | --- |
| A | a domain's IPv4 address |
| AAAA | a domain's IPv6 address |
| CNAME | an alias pointing to another name |
| MX | which mail server receives the domain's email |
| TXT | free-form text — SPF, verification codes |

Your **recursive resolver** does the legwork: it asks a root server,
then the .com TLD servers, then the domain's **authoritative** server,
caching answers on the way. Before all that, the OS checks the local
**hosts file** — a name-to-address override that predates DNS.

## 4. Email and File Transfer

| Protocol | Job | Style |
| --- | --- | --- |
| SMTP | send mail between clients and servers | push |
| POP3 | download to one device, usually deleting from the server | pull, single device |
| IMAP | keep mail on the server, sync folders everywhere | pull, multi-device |

**FTP** moves files in the clear over separate control and data
connections; **SFTP** re-implements transfer over SSH — encrypted,
one channel, one port.

## 5. DHCP: Getting an Address Automatically

@diagram tree id="dhcp-dora" layout="tree" aria="DHCP discover offer request acknowledge exchange"
  "Client broadcasts DISCOVER" -> "Server OFFERS an address"
  "Server OFFERS an address" -> "Client REQUESTS that address"
  "Client REQUESTS that address" -> "Server ACK: lease granted"
@/diagram

The four steps spell **DORA**: Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge.
The address is a lease with an expiry, renewed partway through its
lifetime.

## 6. Threats and Defenses

| Threat | What it is | First line of defense |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Phishing | fake mail or sites harvesting credentials | verify senders, type URLs yourself |
| Malware — virus, worm, trojan, ransomware, spyware | hostile code: copied, self-spread, disguised, extorting, observing | patched OS, antivirus, backups |
| DoS / DDoS | flooding a service offline, often from many hijacked machines | rate limiting, CDNs, scrubbing |
| Man in the middle | intercepting traffic between two parties | TLS everywhere |
| SQL injection | malformed input becomes database commands | parameterized queries |
| Social engineering | manipulating people, not machines | verification procedures, training |

## 7. Firewalls, VPNs, and Authentication

- A **packet-filtering firewall** allows or blocks by IP and port; a
  **stateful firewall** also tracks whole conversations.
- A **VPN** encrypts all traffic to a gateway, shielding it from the
  local network and public Wi-Fi.
- **Two-factor authentication (2FA)** pairs a password with something
  you have or are — a code, a security key, a fingerprint.

## 8. Encryption: Symmetric and Asymmetric

| Property | Symmetric (AES) | Asymmetric (RSA) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Keys | one shared secret | public + private pair |
| Speed | fast | slow |
| Solves | bulk confidentiality | key exchange, signatures |

TLS uses both: an asymmetric handshake to agree on a key, then fast
symmetric AES for the session itself. Add password hygiene — long,
unique passphrases in a manager, never reused — and the stack is
defended end to end.

[summary]
HTTP is stateless — methods, status families, cookies for state. HTTPS
is HTTP inside TLS, with certificates verified against trusted CAs.
DNS records (A, AAAA, CNAME, MX, TXT) resolve through root, TLD, and
authoritative servers. Email is SMTP out, POP3 or IMAP in; DHCP leases
addresses via DORA. Defenses layer: TLS, firewalls, VPNs, 2FA,
parameterized queries, and hybrid encryption.
[/summary]
